course: dictionary
lesson: 4534
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word hear has more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. The sentence «Dogs learn the sound of each car engine, and they hear a dog whistle that is too high for people to hear» uses hear in two ways. Dogs hear sounds with their ears, and people cannot hear the whistle because it is too high for them to notice.
explain: Hear can also mean to be told news. If someone says «I heard you won!», they are not talking about sound. They are saying they were told about your win. Ask yourself: is this about a sound, news, or listening carefully to someone in charge?
explain: Hear can mean to listen formally, as a judge does in a court. Hear also appears in phrases such as hear from, which means to get a message from someone. When you meet hear, try each meaning in the sentence and see which one makes sense.
fact: Hear means to notice sounds with your ears.
fact: Hear can mean to be told news, as in «I heard you won!»
fact: Hear can mean to listen to something formally, as in «The judge heard the case.»
fact: Hear from means to get a message from someone.
fact: Hearing is one of the five senses, and sound travels to your ear and then to your brain.
exercise: «I hear a bird singing outside.» What does hear mean here? | To write something down | *To notice a sound with your ears | To watch something with your eyes
exercise: «I heard you won!» What does heard mean here? | To notice a sound with your ears | *To be told news | To listen to a judge in court
exercise: «The judge heard the case.» What does heard mean here? | *To listen to something formally | To be told news | To notice a sound with your ears
test: In safety, listening helps you cross the road. Why? | *You can hear a car before you can see it | Horns make cars go faster | Cars are always silent, so you must look only | You hear the car only after it has passed
test: Which phrase means to get a message from someone? | hear about the weather | hear of | *hear from
exam: «Reading your work aloud helps you hear missing words, clumsy sentences and tense slips.» What does reading aloud help you do? | Stop hearing your own voice | Hear only the last word on the page | Make your work silent | *Notice missing words and mistakes by listening
